    "created_at": "2019-11-20T21:13:25Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-11-20T21:13:25Z",
    "body": "The Kubernetes module just reads the name of the cluster that is active from the `KUEBCONFIG` file, by default `~/.kube/config`. I think you should be able to achieve what you want by changing the name of the context in that file. It looks like you are using EKS so that should be a case of running `aws eks update-kubeconfig` with the `--alias` flag.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
